WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.430
- It's really good to
see something you know,

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.203
positive happen for Tom Brady.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.470
I'd like to tell you a love story

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.697
it's about president Donald
Trump, and his television.

00:00:12.697 --> 00:00:14.660
Now the billionaire real estate developer,

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.310
turned reality TV star,

00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:18.130
turned president of the United States

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:19.530
and all that really happened.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:20.960
Has had his fair share of ups

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.230
and downs in his seven
plus decades on earth.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:25.600
He's been married three times,

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.780
he's declared bankruptcy six times

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.660
and so on and so forth.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.350
Now only one thing has
been by Trump's side

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.610
all of those years, good old television!

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:38.797
And specifically cable television,

00:00:38.797 --> 00:00:41.880
now Trump has always been both
acutely aware of the power

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.580
of TV and absolutely and
completely addicted to it.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:48.370
A look back at his Twitter
feed easily reveals

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.817
this obsession, quote
"#noratings Lawrence will soon

00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:56.210
"be off tv bad ratings he
has a face made for radio."

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:57.900
Trump treated way back in 2011

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.870
about Lawrence O'Donnell's MSNBC show.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.240
Side bar, it's 2019 and the O'Donnell show

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.760
remains on the air,
that's eight years later.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.070
Quote "Rosie O'Donnell has failed again."

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.457
Trump tweeted in 2012,
"her ratings were abysmal

00:01:09.457 --> 00:01:11.447
"and Oprah canceled her on Friday Night.

00:01:11.447 --> 00:01:14.027
"When will media
executives learn that Rosie

00:01:14.027 --> 00:01:16.550
"just hasn't got it?"

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.120
And then there's this one from 2013

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.140
on Dallas Mavericks owner Mark Cuban.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.617
Quote "@mcuban has less TV persona than

00:01:22.617 --> 00:01:24.377
"any other person I can think of.

00:01:24.377 --> 00:01:26.367
"He's an arrogant, crude, dope

00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:28.993
"who met some very stupid people.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.270
On and on, on and on it goes,

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.970
and the story it tells is
that TV has long been the way

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:39.270
Donald Trump interacts with the world.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.550
The advent of Twitter made
this fact all the more obvious

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.160
over the past decade or so,
but it's hard to believe

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.580
that Trump hasn't always seen himself

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.590
in a sort of give and take conversation

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:51.330
with his television over the years.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.190
Now given that it's utterly predictable

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:57.390
that as he ran for
president he relied deeply

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.460
on TV for his views

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.460
and his what's the word
I'm looking for, expertise?

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:07.490
This exchange from a
Trump appearance in 2015

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.130
on Meet the Press tells you

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.970
all you need to know about that.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.190
- Who do you talk to for
military advice right now?

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:17.240
- Well I watch the shows,
I mean I really see

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:20.230
a lot of great, you know
when you watch your show

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:23.430
and all of the other shows
and you have the generals.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.980
- You have the generals, right,

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:29.260
and Trump's TV watching habits,
even during media interviews

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.630
during the 2016 race would often drive

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.330
the candidate to distraction.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:36.440
In an August 2016 interview
with the Washington Post,

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.510
Trump on at least two
occasions glanced at the TV

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.527
and offered commentary
like, "lot of energy.

00:02:42.527 --> 00:02:43.720
"We got a lot of energy."

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.857
and "That statement's gotten a lot of play

00:02:46.857 --> 00:02:49.477
"I love that statement we wrote."

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:51.500
Distracting right?

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:52.940
Since he arrived in the White House,

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.134
ehh nothing has changed.

00:02:55.134 --> 00:02:57.067
Wrote the New York Times of
Trump in December 2017 quote,

00:02:57.067 --> 00:02:59.657
"People close to him estimate
that Mr. Trump spends

00:02:59.657 --> 00:03:02.977
"at least four hours a
day, and sometimes as much

00:03:02.977 --> 00:03:05.587
"as twice that, in front of a television,

00:03:05.587 --> 00:03:07.267
"sometimes with the volume muted,

00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:10.457
"marinating in the
no-holds-barred wars of cable news

00:03:10.457 --> 00:03:12.980
"and eager to fire back." end quote.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:14.540
In the parlance of the Trump White House

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.280
the president's TV watching
and Tweeting about what he sees

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.690
on TV fits into the category
known as executive time,

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:22.630
and according to schedules
for the past three months

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.100
obtained by Axios there's been a whole lot

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.110
of executive time happening these days.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:32.570
Since the 2018 election
roughly 60%, six zero percent

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:34.340
of Trump's work schedule
has been given over

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:35.663
to executive time.

00:03:36.644 --> 00:03:39.020
That works out to almost
300 hours of executive time

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.760
as compared to approximately
77 hours in which

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.277
Trump has spent in
meetings focused on quote

00:03:44.277 --> 00:03:45.267
"Policy planning, legislative strategy,

00:03:45.267 --> 00:03:47.760
"and video recordings."

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.860
one thing that has changed
since Trump was elected,

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.160
is that he's less willing
now to acknowledge

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.850
just how much TV he actually watches.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:57.427
Quote "Believe it or not,
even when I'm in Washington

00:03:57.427 --> 00:03:59.850
"or New York, I do not
watch much television."

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.607
Trump told reporters aboard
Air Force One in 2017.

00:04:02.607 --> 00:04:04.037
"People that don't know me,

00:04:04.037 --> 00:04:05.617
"they like to say I watch television

00:04:05.617 --> 00:04:07.000
"people with fake sources."

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.257
You know fake reporters, fake sources.

00:04:09.257 --> 00:04:11.347
"But I don't get to watch much television.

00:04:11.347 --> 00:04:13.417
"Primarily because of documents.

00:04:13.417 --> 00:04:14.967
"I'm reading documents.

00:04:14.967 --> 00:04:16.140
"A lot."

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.700
Ah yes the old can't watch
TV because I'm reading

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.610
documents a lot excuse, if
I've heard that one once,

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:24.030
well I've heard that one once.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.160
Here's the problem for Trump,

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:28.540
despite his claims that he
doesn't have time to watch TV

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:31.430
he makes very clear on
an almost weekly basis

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:34.700
how much tele he actually takes in.

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:36.870
In defending one time press
secretary Sean Spicer,

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.647
Trump reportedly told aids quote

00:04:38.647 --> 00:04:40.017
"That guy gets great ratings.

00:04:40.017 --> 00:04:41.800
"Everyone tunes in."

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.070
In explaining why he decided
to pardon conservative writer

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:47.140
and pundit Dinesh D'Souza
last year Trump said this.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:48.397
Quote "I didn't know him.

00:04:48.397 --> 00:04:49.587
"I read the papers.

00:04:49.587 --> 00:04:52.510
"I see him on television,"

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:55.140
and in January alone Trump
Tweeted positive things

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.810
that were said about him, or
backed up his policy positions

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:03.250
on Fox and Friends at least eight times.

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:06.330
It's all very simple, cable
TV is the lens through which

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:08.860
Trump see and understands himself,

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:11.020
the country, and the world.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:16.020
Which may not be the best way
for a president to see things

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:18.623
and that is the point.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:21.810
We make new point episodes twice a week,

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:24.473
check out new ones every
Tuesday and Thursday.

